###### *This Advent and Christmas season our church is getting to experience a few weeks from our Alpha outreach ministry by watching the correlating Alpha videos, then discussing the themes in our small groups. Each blog and video will connect to the Sunday scripture readings, as well as the themes of Advent: Hope, Peace, Joy & Love.* \

{% htmlEmbed
   html="<div style=\"padding:56.25% 0 0 0;position:relative;\">\n  <iframe\n    src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/594961571\"\n    style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\"\n    frameborder=\"0\"\n    allow=\"autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture\"\n    allowfullscreen\n    title=\"Vimeo video\">\n  </iframe>\n</div>" /%}

My experience from the very first Alpha course that I went through had one of the biggest impacts in my life. The Alpha Weekend (or Alpha Day as it was in this case) is all about the Holy Spirit. We watch about 4 videos in one day, but have time for prayer after one of the videos. I remember walking back to the prayer team and being prayed over. They prayed “Come Holy Spirit”, and then we waited. One of the things mentioned in the talks was that the gift of tongues is often one of the first supernatural gifts that are evident. So they prayed over me to receive the gift of tongues. There were no words that were uttered on my part. They encouraged me to speak out loud. I said, “I’ve got nothing to say.” They asked me if I was feeling anything different. I said “peace”.&nbsp;

Previously in my life, peace was something that has always seemed to escape me. Instead, I was usually filled with anxiety. This peace I experienced in this moment on the retreat was unlike anything I had ever felt before. This time, I did not have performance anxiety to pretend that I had a gift that I didn’t. That day, I didn’t have anxiety that something was wrong with me because I did not receive the gift of tongues. I had a peace that spread from my insides all the way across my face in a smile and tears of joy. I could have sat back in that seat all day and night if time allowed for it, just resting in the peace of Jesus. And going forward, I had never felt such freedom as I did the few weeks after that. God had broken chains I didn’t realize I had been bound in for so long. I felt like a new person. I was not filled with worry or anxiety over small things anymore. I had a peace beyond understanding. I had an encounter with God that day that I will never forget, and it set me on a trajectory that is still unfolding.&nbsp;

As I’ve looked back on that day through the years I can clearly see how beautifully the Holy Spirit was moving in my life. He produced that peace within me as I sat in silence with Him. The closeness I experienced was unlike any peace this world can offer. It was a gift of the Holy Spirit, and it reminds me of what we celebrate during the second week of Advent: the peace that comes from God’s presence with us.

This week's Alpha episode is titled *What Does the Holy Spirit Do?* During the episode, one of the scriptures mentioned is Romans 8:14-15, “For you did not receive the spirit of slavery to fall back into fear, but you have received the Spirit of adoption as sons, by whom we cry ‘Abba! Father.’”There is such a beauty in that close relationship that we receive in Christ through the Holy Spirit. Our Old Testament reading this week says “He will tend his flock like a shepherd; he will gather the lambs in his arms; he will carry them in his bosom, and gently lead those that are with young.” What a loving and close relationship that is; He holds us tenderly, close to his heart, and leads us.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Our Gospel reading on Sunday quotes part of the Old Testament reading speaking about John the Baptist preparing the way for Jesus to come. John baptized with water, but Jesus baptizes in the Holy Spirit. And our New Testament reading in 2 Peter 3:14-15a says “Therefore beloved, since you are waiting for these [new heaven and new earth] be diligent to be found by him without spot or blemish, and at peace. And count the patience of our Lord as salvation…”. While we are in our waiting period there are still struggles, but we can be at peace because we are filled with the Holy Spirit. How can this be? Galatians 5:22-23 tells us “But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control; against such things there is no law.” The Spirit gives us these gifts, and we are called to live our lives fully into them; for verse 25 states “If we live by the Spirit, let us also keep in step with the Spirit.”

So this week in Advent where we focus on peace, let yourself be immersed in the Holy Spirit. Take time to meditate in his word, and be filled with his precious beauty and gifts.&nbsp;
